1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,243
Blast radius is not a term from AI, it comes from systems engineering.

2
00:00:04,243 --> 00:00:08,859
Before anyone ships a change, they ask what breaks and how far the break spreads if

3
00:00:08,859 --> 00:00:10,639
the worst case actually happens.

4
00:00:10,639 --> 00:00:15,033
It is a useful habit to steal, because most people setting up their first agent

5
00:00:15,033 --> 00:00:16,145
never ask it at all.

6
00:00:16,145 --> 00:00:17,591
They ask whether it works.

7
00:00:17,591 --> 00:00:20,316
They do not ask what happens the day it does not.

8
00:00:20,316 --> 00:00:24,933
For the agents in this course, the worst version comes in three flavours, and it is

9
00:00:24,933 --> 00:00:27,435
worth naming them in ascending order of pain.

10
00:00:27,435 --> 00:00:31,440
It reads something it should never have seen: uncomfortable, and usually

11
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:32,107
recoverable.

12
00:00:32,107 --> 00:00:36,167
It sends something it should never have sent: public, and apology shaped.

13
00:00:36,167 --> 00:00:40,505
Or it quietly changes a number somewhere nobody looks: the worst of the three,

14
00:00:40,505 --> 00:00:45,122
because the first two announce themselves and this one waits until the wrong figure

15
00:00:45,122 --> 00:00:47,458
has been in front of a client for a month.

16
00:00:47,458 --> 00:00:51,963
Every limit this lecture sets exists to shrink one of those three, and the silent

17
00:00:51,963 --> 00:00:55,911
one is why read only is a real permission level and not a technicality.

18
00:00:56,461 --> 00:00:58,113
Those are different questions.

19
00:00:58,113 --> 00:01:00,591
What you ask it to do is the job description.

20
00:01:00,591 --> 00:01:05,051
What it is able to reach is the access you granted when you connected an account,

21
00:01:05,051 --> 00:01:07,749
and access is almost always broader than the job.

22
00:01:07,749 --> 00:01:12,264
An inbox connection that can read one folder can usually read every folder, unless

23
00:01:12,264 --> 00:01:13,751
you deliberately narrow it.

24
00:01:13,751 --> 00:01:18,266
The gap between the two is rarely visible at setup, because permission screens are

25
00:01:18,266 --> 00:01:20,799
written in the provider's language, not yours.

26
00:01:20,799 --> 00:01:24,653
"Read your mail" means every folder, every year, attachments included.

27
00:01:24,653 --> 00:01:29,003
"Manage your calendar" usually includes deleting events, not just reading them.

28
00:01:29,003 --> 00:01:33,518
"Edit spreadsheets" means every sheet the account can open, not the one you had in

29
00:01:33,518 --> 00:01:33,794
mind.

30
00:01:33,794 --> 00:01:38,254
The practical habit: read the permission screen as a list of what you are handing

31
00:01:38,254 --> 00:01:42,053
over, not as a formality, and where a narrower grant exists, take it.

32
00:01:42,053 --> 00:01:46,513
Where one does not, the narrowing has to happen on your side instead: a dedicated

33
00:01:46,513 --> 00:01:50,588
folder, a label, a copy of the sheet that holds only what the agent needs.

34
00:01:50,588 --> 00:01:54,497
Priya's Renewals label is exactly that move, and it costs five minutes.

35
00:01:55,047 --> 00:01:59,754
Priya's follow up agent, built in Section 6, is scoped to her Renewals folder alone

36
00:01:59,754 --> 00:02:01,398
and drafts only, never sends.

37
00:02:01,398 --> 00:02:03,496
That is not caution for its own sake.

38
00:02:03,496 --> 00:02:08,032
It is the exact reason a contained incident later in this course stays contained

39
00:02:08,032 --> 00:02:12,625
instead of becoming a real one: the access was narrow before anything went wrong,

40
00:02:12,625 --> 00:02:14,439
not narrowed afterward as a fix.

41
00:02:14,439 --> 00:02:19,089
The ceiling deserves a moment, because it is the limit people set last and lean on

42
00:02:19,089 --> 00:02:19,373
most.

43
00:02:19,373 --> 00:02:24,022
A ceiling is always a number: never more than this many messages in a day, never a

44
00:02:24,022 --> 00:02:28,672
figure above this amount touched without a human, never an email outside this list

45
00:02:28,672 --> 00:02:29,409
of addresses.

46
00:02:29,409 --> 00:02:31,677
Numbers matter because they fail loudly.

47
00:02:31,677 --> 00:02:36,440
"Be sensible about volume" cannot trip an alarm; "more than twenty drafts in an hour

48
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:40,919
means stop" can, and an agent stuck in a loop is exactly the failure that turns

49
00:02:40,919 --> 00:02:43,811
twenty into two hundred while you are in a meeting.

50
00:02:43,811 --> 00:02:45,966
Pick ceilings you expect never to hit.

51
00:02:45,966 --> 00:02:48,177
Their job is not to manage normal days.

52
00:02:48,177 --> 00:02:52,657
Their job is to make an abnormal day cost you an alert instead of an afternoon.

53
00:02:53,207 --> 00:02:55,806
Treat this as a literal gate, not an aspiration.

54
00:02:55,806 --> 00:03:00,300
The whole diagram costs perhaps ten minutes of writing, and every path that skips a

55
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:03,170
question lands in the same place: do not connect yet.

56
00:03:03,170 --> 00:03:06,418
If that feels strict, notice what it is protecting you from.

57
00:03:06,418 --> 00:03:08,097
Nobody regrets the ten minutes.

58
00:03:08,097 --> 00:03:12,591
The regrets in this course all belong to accounts connected first and thought about

59
00:03:12,591 --> 00:03:15,948
afterwards, and Section 8's near miss is exactly one of those.

60
00:03:16,498 --> 00:03:18,685
Why be that untrusting about intentions?

61
00:03:18,685 --> 00:03:22,238
Because an agent's input is also, functionally, its instructions.

62
00:03:22,238 --> 00:03:26,502
Everything it reads becomes part of what it thinks about, including text other

63
00:03:26,502 --> 00:03:27,212
people wrote.

64
00:03:27,212 --> 00:03:29,617
An email can, in effect, talk to your agent.

65
00:03:29,617 --> 00:03:31,476
Most of the time that is harmless.

66
00:03:31,476 --> 00:03:35,740
Occasionally it is exactly how an agent ends up doing something nobody in your

67
00:03:35,740 --> 00:03:36,778
building asked for.

68
00:03:36,778 --> 00:03:39,074
You do not need to master that risk today.

69
00:03:39,074 --> 00:03:43,666
You need the design habit that makes it survivable, which is the same one this whole

70
00:03:43,666 --> 00:03:47,383
lecture teaches: what it can reach is the risk, so narrow the reach.

71
00:03:47,383 --> 00:03:51,811
That sentence is the whole argument for Section 8, which comes back to this exact

72
00:03:51,811 --> 00:03:53,287
idea with a real near miss.

73
00:03:53,287 --> 00:03:57,605
For now, one habit: set scope, action and ceiling before you connect, not after

74
00:03:57,605 --> 00:03:58,753
something goes wrong.

75
00:03:59,303 --> 00:04:03,605
If step two turned up something, you have already found your first real guardrail.

76
00:04:03,605 --> 00:04:08,011
Next lecture turns all of this, the scope, the trial, the ceiling, into one document

77
00:04:08,011 --> 00:04:10,162
you write before you build anything else.
